<Document>
  <name>Alpine MTB Map</name>
  <visibility>1</visibility>
  <atom:author><atom:name>Vincent Emonet and contributors</atom:name></atom:author>
  <atom:link href="https://github.com/vemonet/alpine-mtb-map" rel="related"/>
  <description><![CDATA[A curated open map for gravity mountain biking. Discover top downhill bike parks, enduro trails, and freeride spots worldwide. Each main pin gives what it costs to get up, what the trails are like, and the weather forecast. Blue pins are purpose-built bike parks, dark green pins are natural lift-served spots, and brown pins are the ones you pedal up. A few spots stay ridable in winter. Lines are trail geometries from OpenStreetMap - treat them as indicative, not as a substitute for on-site signage. Compiled July 2026.<br/><br/>
    <b>Licence: Open Database License (ODbL) v1.0</b><br/>
    This file is a derivative database of OpenStreetMap - the trail geometries were extracted from OpenStreetMap ways, relations and public GPS traces - so the database as a whole is released under the same share-alike terms. Each trail line names the OpenStreetMap object it came from in its own description.<br/><br/>
    Data &#169; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a>, ODbL 1.0.<br/>
    Spot data &#169; 2026 Vincent Emonet and contributors, ODbL 1.0 - <a href="https://github.com/vemonet/alpine-mtb-map">github.com/vemonet/alpine-mtb-map</a>.<br/>
    Full licence text: <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/">opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0</a>.<br/><br/>
    You are free to share, create and adapt, as long as you <b>attribute</b> (keep this notice in any public use of the database or of works produced from it), <b>share alike</b> (offer any adapted database under the ODbL) and <b>keep it open</b> (no DRM-only redistribution).<br/><br/>
    The individual contents - descriptive text, prices, travel times and trail notes - are additionally available under the <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/dbcl/1-0/">Database Contents License (DbCL) v1.0</a>, mirroring how OpenStreetMap separates its database licence from its contents licence.<br/><br/>
    Trail lines are simplified and indicative only - follow the signs on the ground. Prices, schedules, trail status and access change; verify with the operator before travelling.]]></description>
