  <Placemark>
    <name>Carvins Cove (Roanoke, Virginia) [7 USD parking]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Carvins Cove 359 m - Bennett Springs trailhead, 13,000 acres above Roanoke</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The centrepiece of Virginia's Blue Ridge, the first IMBA Silver-Level Ride Center on the East Coast. Carvins Cove Natural Reserve is one of the largest municipal parks in the United States: roughly 60 miles of trail around the reservoir, about 32 miles of it singletrack, mixing fire road, cross-country singletrack and a set of genuine freeride downhill lines. The steep stuff sits on the ridges - Gauntlet, Rattlin' Run, Jacob's Drop, Upper and Lower Comet, Hi-Dee-Hoe and the Trough Expert line - while Enchanted Forest, Arrowhead, Hemlock Tunnel and Songbird link the network together lower down. OpenStreetMap carries IMBA difficulty grades on much of it.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Riding is free; parking is 7 USD a day or 50 USD a year at the two lots riders use, Bennett Springs and Timberview. Cash or check at the lot, or buy online from the Western Virginia Water Authority in advance.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Shared with hikers, trail runners and horses on most trails. The reserve is Roanoke's drinking-water catchment: no swimming, and stay on the trail.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round; the Blue Ridge stays open through mild winters. Summer is humid and the clay holds water after storms.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Roanoke</td><td>Car</td><td>~20 min</td></tr><tr><td>Richmond</td><td>Car</td><td>~3h00</td></tr><tr><td>Washington DC</td><td>Car</td><td>~4h00</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.visitroanokeva.com/biking/mountain-biking/carvins-cove/">Visit Virginia's Blue Ridge - Carvins Cove</a>, <a href="https://www.westernvawater.org/learn/recreation-options-shelter-reservations/hiking-mountain-biking-horseback-riding">Western Virginia Water Authority recreation pages</a> and OpenStreetMap. Fees and access change; verify before travelling.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-79.947000,37.383810,359</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">carvins-cove</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner intermediate expert natural no-lift dh enduro freeride</mwm:value><mwm:value key="price_day">7 USD</mwm:value><mwm:value key="price_season">50 USD</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Sport</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Buffalo Creek (Colorado) [free, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Little Scraggy trailhead 2394 m - decomposed-granite flow in the Pike National Forest</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>An IMBA Epic and the Front Range's fast-riding classic, an hour from Denver. The 2002 Hayman Fire opened the forest up, and what it left is a network of buff, sandy, decomposed-granite singletrack that carries speed better than almost anything else in Colorado. Little Scraggy, Sandy Wash, Nice Kitty, Skipper, Homestead, Charlies Cutoff, Gashouse Gulch, Green Mountain and Strawberry Jack link into loops of any length, with Colorado Trail segments 2 and 3 running through. Blackjack (mapped as Raspberry Ridge / Blackjack) is the outlier: big rock rolls and tight lines through boulders, genuinely technical where the rest is flowing. Everything sits between roughly 2200 and 2600 m, so altitude is a real factor.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no fee - this is National Forest land. Ride from the Little Scraggy, Buffalo Creek or Baldy trailheads; loops are pedalled, and the network is laid out so the climbs are gradual and the descents long.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Burn-scar terrain at 2400 m: little shade, afternoon thunderstorms in summer, and no water on the trails. Carry more than you think.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Typically dry and rideable from May into October; snow closes it through the winter and the sandy tread is at its best a day or two after rain.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Denver</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h00</td></tr><tr><td>Colorado Springs</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h30</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.evo.com/blogs/travel-guides/buffalo-creek-colorado-mountain-bike-trails">evo Buffalo Creek trail guide</a>, <a href="https://www.singletracks.com/bike-trails/buffalo-creek/">Singletracks</a> and OpenStreetMap. Forest closures follow fire risk; check before travelling.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-105.257380,39.343660,2394</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">buffalo-creek-co</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner intermediate expert natural no-lift enduro freeride</mwm:value><mwm:value key="open_from">05-01</mwm:value><mwm:value key="closed_from">11-01</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Sport</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
