  <Placemark>
    <name>Cady Hill Forest - Stowe (Vermont) [free, donation]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Cady Hill Forest 282 m - Stowe Trails Partnership's flagship network</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>320 acres owned by the Town of Stowe under a Stowe Land Trust conservation easement, and one of the most ridden networks in the northeast. About 11 miles of single and double track packed into a small hill, ranging from mellow to properly steep. Florence - "Flo" - is entirely machine-built and bike-only, a fast line of high berms and small-to-moderate tabletops; Bear's is one of the oldest trails here and now rides somewhere between a classic technical descent and a modern freeride rollercoaster. Charlie's, Chuck's Corkscrew, Growler and the Hare Line fill out the rest. It sits right on the Stowe Rec Path, so you can ride to it from the village.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no gate. The trails are free to ride; Stowe Trails Partnership and the Vermont Mountain Bike Association fund the maintenance through membership, and joining is the expected courtesy rather than a fee.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Vermont mud season: the trails close every spring until the ground firms up, and STP posts the reopening. Riding soft Vermont singletrack ruins it for the season.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Typically late May through October, with the exact opening set by mud season each year and posted by Stowe Trails Partnership.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Stowe village</td><td>Bike</td><td>~10 min</td></tr><tr><td>Burlington</td><td>Car</td><td>~50 min</td></tr><tr><td>Boston</td><td>Car</td><td>~3h30</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://stowetrails.org/cady-hill-forest/">Stowe Trails Partnership</a>, <a href="https://vmba.org/plan_your_ride/cady-hill-forest/">VMBA</a> and OpenStreetMap. Mud-season closures change yearly; check STP before riding.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
