  <Placemark>
    <name>Raccoon Mountain (Chattanooga, Tennessee) [free, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Switch Yard trailhead 510 m - 30+ miles around the TVA reservoir above the river gorge</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>One of the pillars of Chattanooga's riding, on Tennessee Valley Authority land encircling the Raccoon Mountain pumped-storage reservoir, with the Tennessee River Gorge dropping away below. Over 30 miles of trail, colour-graded green through black, and classic southern Appalachian in character: rocky, technical, switchbacked. The gravity interest is on the eastern rim, where Live Wire 1 (1.8 mi) and Live Wire 2 (3.1 mi), Megawatt (2.6 mi) and High Voltage fall off the plateau towards the gorge, with Electric Avenue and the moderate River Gorge trail alongside and a marked free-ride area on the mountain.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no fee - TVA keeps it open to the public. Pedal from the Switch Yard or East Overlook car parks on the reservoir rim.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; The gorge-side descents are exposed and rocky, and the trails are shared with hikers. TVA closes sections for plant work without much notice.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round in a typical year; the rock drains well but the trails close after heavy rain.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Chattanooga</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Nashville</td><td>Car</td><td>~2h00</td></tr><tr><td>Atlanta</td><td>Car</td><td>~2h00</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.tva.com/environment/recreation/mountain-biking">TVA recreation</a>, <a href="https://www.singletracks.com/bike-trails/raccoon-mountain-trail-network/">Singletracks</a> and OpenStreetMap. Verify trail status with TVA before travelling.</i>
    <br/><small>Five lines are drawn, all graded from OpenStreetMap's own IMBA difficulty tags. Megawatt is mapped but is not drawn: OSM holds it as a 4.1 km rim traverse dropping 69 m at 1.7%, so it does not read as a descent, and the same is true of Electric Avenue, Grindstone Ridge, Laurel Point and the marked free-ride area. The bbox also contains the Tennessee River Gorge hiking network and the climbers' approach trails to the T-Wall crag, which are not bike trails and were excluded by name.</small>]]></description>
