  <Placemark>
    <name>Trione-Annadel State Park (Santa Rosa, CA) [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Channel Drive entrance, ~120 m (~400 ft)</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Pedal-accessed state park with 35-45 miles of multi-use trails through volcanic rock, basalt outcrops and old cobblestone quarry terrain. Riding mixes oak woodland, grassland, chaparral and riparian zones. Lake Ilsanjo and Ledson Marsh sit inside the park; Bennett Mountain tops out at 575 m (1,887 ft). Popular routes include the Canyon Trail, South Burma and ridges above Lake Ilsanjo. Natural, rocky and loose volcanic surfaces mean the riding suits confident intermediates and experts; a few easier fire roads and the Channel Trail are more beginner-friendly.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. You pedal up the fire roads and ridge trails. Vehicle day-use fee is $8 (regular auto) paid at the Visitor Center on Channel Drive or via the LAZ app; several California State Parks annual passes are accepted. No camping inside the park; nearest camping is Spring Lake Regional Park and Sugarloaf Ridge State Park. E-bikes are considered motorized vehicles and are not allowed on trails.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open year-round 8 AM to sunset, no overnight use. Best riding is spring through fall; summers are warm and winters are cool and muddy after rain. Peak wildflower season is April to June.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Santa Rosa</td><td>Car / bike</td><td>~10 min</td></tr><tr><td>San Francisco</td><td>Car</td><td>~1 h 15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Kenwood (Lawndale Rd trailhead)</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min to park</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=480">California State Parks - Trione-Annadel State Park</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trione-Annadel_State_Park">Wikipedia - Trione-Annadel State Park</a>. Dogs are only allowed on paved areas; check current trail closures and dam-work notices before visiting.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
