  <Placemark>
    <name>Sorata MTB (Bolivia) [jeep shuttle]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Paso Illampu 4724 m - shuttle drop for the Sorata descents, Bolivia</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Some of the longest gravity descents anywhere, under the 6000 m faces of Illampu and Ancohuma at the north end of the Cordillera Real. Jeeps carry riders to passes above 4500 m and the runs drop around 2000 m to the valley - the line traced here falls 1922 m over 13.6 km from Paso Illampu down to Sorata at 2800 m, on unpaved tracks and footpaths through abandoned high farming villages. Beyond the day descents sit some serious multi-day routes: the Camino del Oro or Gold Digger's Way, 60 km down towards Tipuani, the Mapiri trail, the Illampu Circuit and the trans-Andean crossing to La Paz.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/><b>No lift - jeep shuttle.</b> There is no published day rate; riding is arranged through operators in Sorata or La Paz, and Gravity Bolivia runs multi-day trips here. A local guide is close to essential - almost none of the riding is mapped, the descents cross working farming communities, and the altitude is unforgiving if anything goes wrong.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>The Andean dry season, roughly May to September, is the reliable window. The wet season from November to March makes the tracks difficult and the high passes unpleasant.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Sorata</td><td>Jeep shuttle up</td><td>~1 h 30</td></tr><tr><td>La Paz</td><td>Car / bus to Sorata</td><td>~4 h</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.gravitybolivia.com/rides-2/multi-day/spectacular-sorata/">Gravity Bolivia - Spectacular Sorata</a>, <a href="https://www.gravitybolivia.com/rides-2/">Gravity Bolivia - rides</a> and <a href="https://360.sorata.bo/">Sorata tourism</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-68.566855,-15.759320,4724</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">bolivia-sorata</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">expert natural enduro dh no-lift</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Cerro Áspero MTB (Rocha, Uruguay) [no lift, event-marked]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Cerro Áspero 235 m - summit of the Rocha sierra, where the marked descent starts</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The emblematic ride of the Sierras de Rocha, and the format is unusual: you push and carry the bike to the summit for the view, then descend a roughly 40 km circuit marked for the occasion with arrows, ribbons, stakes and flags. Natural rolling grazing country - low altitude, but genuinely remote, with no signage outside event weekends. The Travesía al Cerro Áspero has run for more than a decade and passed its eleventh edition, and the organisers lay on meals (the traditional cazuela), hydration stations, showers, lodging and emergency cover for the day.<br/><small>Nothing is drawn here: an mtb-tag sweep of the sierra returns zero features, and the circuit only exists as markings put out for the event.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no shuttle - the climb is on foot. Free to ride the hills; the organised traverse charges an entry that covers the food and support.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round; the organised traverse runs in the Uruguayan spring or autumn, so check the calendar if you want the circuit marked.<br/>Daily opening hours: daylight hours<br/>
    <small>Open grazing country on private land in places; ask locally and go with somebody who knows the lines.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access">
      <tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Rocha</td><td>Car</td><td>~1 h</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Montevideo</td><td>Car</td><td>~3 h</td></tr>
    </table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://turismorocha.gub.uy/atractivos/deportes/ciclismo-y-mountain-bike">Rocha Tourism on cycling and mountain biking</a> for the format and the 40 km circuit, and the <a href="https://mtb.uy/temas/rocha-cerro-%C3%81spero-edici%C3%B3n-11.47/">Mountain Bike Uruguay thread on the eleventh edition</a>. Pin moved onto the OSM <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/2801279375">Cerro Aspero</a> peak node (2801279375), measured at 237 m; the previous coordinate was rounded to two decimals in both axes and sat 40 km away. Trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-54.505616,-34.445151,237</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">uruguay-cerro-aspero</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner expert natural enduro</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Santos Trailhead / Cross Florida Greenway [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[IMBA Ride Center and extensive singletrack network around Santos, with more than 80 miles of mountain-bike trails, a pump track and skills park. Open year-round during park hours. <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.floridastateparks.org/learn/biking-cross-florida-greenway">Florida State Parks biking guide</a> and <a href="https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/santos-trailhead-campground/experiences-amenities">Santos Trailhead information</a>.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-82.132000,29.166000,31</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">us-florida-santos</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner expert natural enduro</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Alafia River State Park MTB [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[One of Florida's most varied mountain-bike areas, built through reclaimed phosphate-mining terrain with unusually steep elevation changes for the state. Park trails are maintained and conditions can change; check closures before riding. <i>Source: <a href="https://www.floridastateparks.org/Alafia">Florida State Parks - Alafia River</a>. Pin placed on the OSM-mapped <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/383705336">bike trailhead parking</a> (way 383705336), measured at 31 m; the previous coordinate was 5 km north-west, in the C.W. Bill Young Regional Reservoir.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-82.138210,27.785190,31</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">us-florida-alafia</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner expert natural enduro</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Markham Park MTB Trails (Florida) [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Managed mountain-bike trail network at Markham Park in Broward County, with purpose-built trails and riding facilities. Check the park page for current access, conditions and rules. <i>Source: <a href="https://markhampark.com/biking/">Markham Park mountain-bike trails</a>.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-80.360000,26.118000,2</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">us-florida-markham</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner expert natural enduro no-lift</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>

  <Placemark>
    <name>Fruita MTB - 18 Road and Kokopelli [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Fruita / Grand Valley desert singletrack destination</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Two adjoining, world-known riding areas: the North Fruita Desert / 18 Road system for fast, playful desert singletrack, and the Kokopelli Trail system near Loma for exposed ledges, slickrock, technical loops and long views over the Colorado River. Classics include Joe's Ridge, Kessel Run, Mary's Loop, Horsethief Bench and Rustler's Loop.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Pedal-accessed trailheads and desert roads; check land-manager rules, parking requirements and closures before riding.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round when trails are dry. Avoid muddy conditions because desert clay can be damaged easily; summer heat requires an early start and extra water.<br/><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://gofruita.com/mountain-biking-trails/">Go Fruita trail overview</a>, <a href="https://www.recreation.gov/gateways/16583">BLM / Recreation.gov Kokopelli Trails</a> and <a href="https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/james-m-robb-colorado-river-state-park/james-m-robb-colorado-river-state-park-fruita">Colorado Parks and Wildlife - Fruita</a>.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-108.706010,39.310380,1551</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app"><mwm:properties><mwm:value key="spot">us-fruita-mtb</mwm:value><mwm:value key="tags">beginner expert natural no-lift enduro freeride</mwm:value><mwm:value key="open_from">01-01</mwm:value><mwm:value key="closed_from">01-01</mwm:value></mwm:properties><mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility></ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Flagstaff MTB Network (Arizona) [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Rocky Ridge Trail Parking 2195 m - Schultz Creek trailhead at the north-west foot of Mount Elden, Flagstaff, Arizona</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>A broad network of singletrack and multi-use trails around Flagstaff, including the Arizona Trail, Schultz Creek, Elden and the urban trail system. The high-altitude pine forest provides varied cross-country and natural all-mountain riding, with volcanic terrain and long seasonal views. The pin marks the Schultz Creek trailhead at the bottom of Schultz Pass Road, the busiest access on the network: Schultz Creek Trail, Rocky Ridge, Lower Oldham and the Arizona Trail connectors all leave from here, and the Mount Elden and Little Elden descents are reached from the same road.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Ride from signed trailheads and use the current forest and city maps. Some routes are shared-use and some Arizona Trail sections have local restrictions, so follow posted rules.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Lower trails can ride much of the year, while snow and freeze-thaw affect higher routes in winter and spring. Check current closures and trail conditions before riding.<br/><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.flagstaff.com/flagstaff-mountain-biking">Flagstaff mountain biking guide</a>, <a href="https://flagstaff.az.gov/1521/Flagstaff-Urban-Trails-Bikeways-Map">City of Flagstaff trail map</a> and <a href="https://flagstafftrails.com/">Flagstaff Biking Organization</a>. Pin on the OSM-mapped <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/956438874">Rocky Ridge Trail Parking</a> (way 956438874), measured at 2195 m. The previous pin sat in central Flagstaff, 5.7 km from any trailhead.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
