  <Placemark>
    <name>Coed y Brenin (Wales) [free, parking charge]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Coed y Brenin 135 m - visitor centre and trailhead, north of Dolgellau</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Britain's first purpose-built trail centre, and the place the whole trail-centre idea started: Tarw Du, cut in 1996, is the oldest purpose-built mountain bike trail in the country. Around 100 km of all-weather waymarked singletrack now runs through the Eryri forest, from a short green through the blue MinorTaur - built to be genuinely inclusive, adaptive bikes included - to the red Tarw Du and Dragon's Back and the 38 km Beast of Brenin at the black end. Rock-armoured Welsh singletrack that rides the same in December as in July, which is the point.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no shuttle; every trail is a pedal loop from the visitor centre. Riding is free, car parking is charged by Natural Resources Wales. Cafe, bike shop, hire and showers on site.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Year-round, and deliberately all-weather - the surfaced trails drain and hold up through Welsh winters.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Dolgellau</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Manchester</td><td>Car</td><td>~3h00</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://naturalresources.wales/days-out/places-to-visit/north-west-wales/coed-y-brenin-visitor-centre/?lang=en">Natural Resources Wales</a>, <a href="https://www.mbwales.com/agents/coed-y-brenin/">MB Wales</a> and <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/">OpenStreetMap</a>. Prices, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
