  <Placemark>
    <name>Duthie Hill Bike Park (Washington, USA) [free, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Issaquah 145 m - Duthie Hill Park car park on the Sammamish Plateau</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Fifty hectares of dense evergreen forest half an hour from Seattle, laid out as a four-leaf clover of trail zones around a central clearing - and one of the most influential volunteer-built parks in the United States. About 10 km of trail packs in freeride lines, progressive jump lines, flow trails, pump tracks and skills features, with a clear beginner-to-expert ladder. Ryan's Eternal Flow and the Voodoo Child freeride line are the names people come for. Built and maintained with Evergreen Mountain Bike Alliance.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift; the hill is small and every line is a short pedal back up. Free, on King County parkland.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Year-round, daylight hours. Pacific Northwest wet from October to May - the jump lines get closed when they are too soft to ride without damage.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Issaquah</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Seattle</td><td>Car</td><td>~35 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://kingcounty.gov/en/dept/dnrp/nature-recreation/parks-recreation/king-county-parks/parks/duthie-hill">King County Parks</a>, <a href="https://www.evergreenmtb.org/">Evergreen Mountain Bike Alliance</a> and <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/">OpenStreetMap</a>. Trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-blue</styleUrl>
