  <Placemark>
    <name>Yungas Road - Death Road (Bolivia) [80-130 USD guided]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>La Cumbre 4640 m - the pass where the descent starts, above La Paz</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The most-ridden famous descent in South America, and not a trail network but one road: 64 km from the La Cumbre pass down to Coroico, losing about 3,450 m through four climate zones - icy altiplano, then cloud forest, then humid jungle. The first stretch is sealed; the notorious section is the old single-lane gravel shelf cut into the cliff, with no guard rail and drops of several hundred metres, which earned the road its name when it still carried two-way traffic. Traffic now uses the new highway, so the descent is effectively closed to vehicles.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no climbing - operators drive riders from La Paz to the pass and pick up at the bottom. Dozens of companies run it; a guided day is typically 80-130 USD with bike, full-face helmet, guide and support vehicle. Bike and brake quality varies enormously between operators and this is the one place where that matters.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Year-round. The dry season, May to October, has the best visibility; in the wet season the gravel section is muddy, foggy and considerably more serious.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>La Paz</td><td>Operator van</td><td>~1h00 to the pass</td></tr><tr><td>Coroico</td><td>Car</td><td>~3h00 back to La Paz</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yungas_Road">Yungas Road</a>, operator route descriptions and <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/">OpenStreetMap</a>. Prices, road status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
