  <Placemark>
    <name>Homewood Mountain Resort (Lake Tahoe, California, USA) [no-lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Homewood 1903 m - ten free miles above the West Shore</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>A network of roughly ten miles of trails inside the resort boundary on Lake Tahoe's West Shore, made up of singletrack, fire roads, old logging trails and access roads, with what are arguably the best lake views of any riding in the basin. Free to use; riders are asked to stay on designated trails to protect on-mountain restoration work. Good for an afternoon of descending rather than a destination in its own right.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift for bikes - pedal up. No fee for the trail network. A summer retail and rental shop at the North Lodge rents bikes, and a summer trail map is published by the resort.<br/><br/><small><b>Check before travelling.</b> Homewood sat out the 2024-25 winter entirely during a redevelopment dispute, reopened for winter 2025-26 after committing to remain open to the public, and is due to break ground on a new 8-passenger gondola in summer 2026 - so on-mountain access may be disrupted.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open from June once the snow clears; closed from October.<br/>Daily opening hours: dawn to dusk<br/><small>High Sierra at ~1,900 m - snow lingers on the upper trails well into June.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Tahoe City</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Truckee</td><td>Car</td><td>~30 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Source: <a href="https://skihomewood.com/">Homewood Mountain Resort</a>.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
