  <Placemark>
    <name>Monte Cornizzolo (Valmadrera and Civate, Lecco) [no lift, pedal up]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Monte Cornizzolo 1241 m - the summit the descents drop from</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The first real summit north of the Brianza plain, rising straight out of Lake Annone above Valmadrera and Civate, with a dense web of natural descents falling off it in every direction. Seventeen lines are drawn here, from the 3.1 km <b>Pozzo DH</b>, which loses 720 m from 1109 m down to the hamlet of Pozzo above Civate at an average 23%, and <b>Sentiero nr 7</b>, 2.5 km for 585 m, through mid-length technical lines like <b>La Crota - I Casei #6</b> and <b>Val Gatton</b>, to short freeride variants off the Cornizzolo shoulder and the gentle <b>Antica via Romana</b> along the bottom. Terrain is limestone: fixed and loose rock, roots, tight tree-lined turns, with the harder lines steep and continuously technical rather than flowy. The northern trails run over towards the Corni di Canzo and Val Ravella, so the network reaches well beyond Cornizzolo itself.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no shuttle service: everything here is earned. A paved road climbs from the Eupilio side to around 900 m on the Val Pesora flank, and published route descriptions note a gate on the upper section that has to be passed on foot with the bike. The usual approach is to pedal from the valley - the marked trailhead parking above Civate sits at 307 m, so a full lap from there is a 900 m climb. Free.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open from 1 March; closed from 1 December (estimated snow-free indication).<br/>Daily opening hours: daylight only<br/><small>These are public mountain paths, not a bike park: nothing here is built, signed or maintained for gravity riding, and walkers and paragliders use the same ground. Two of the drawn lines cross the <b>Riserva Naturale Sasso Malascarpa</b>, where reserve rules apply and were not verified for this map - check at the visitor centre before riding them. Snow and ice can sit on the summit slopes into spring; the limestone gets greasy in the wet.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Lecco</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Milan</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h</td></tr><tr><td>Milan</td><td>Train to Civate or Valmadrera</td><td>~1h15</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: all seventeen trail lines were drawn in gpx.studio and supplied for this map, then simplified to ~8 m; they are not operator or tourism-office exports. Pin on the OpenStreetMap summit node <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/457470732">457470732</a> ("Monte Cornizzolo"), tagged 1241 m. Area and access detail from published Italian MTB route guides for Monte Cornizzolo.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>9.301933,45.837066,1241</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://comaps.app">
