  <Placemark>
    <name>Imlil (High Atlas, Morocco) [no lift, guided shuttle]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Imlil 1720 m - the Toubkal trailhead village and the region's riding base</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Imlil sits at the head of the Mizane valley below Jbel Toubkal, at 4167 m the highest peak in North Africa, and works as a base rather than a single hill: guides shuttle to a pass and you descend mule tracks, irrigation-channel paths and old village trails back down. Twelve lines are drawn here. Around the village itself are short, sharp links between the Berber hamlets - <b>Downhill Targa Imoula</b>, <b>Downhill Ait Souka</b>, <b>Go in town</b>, <b>Back intown</b>, the flat <b>Inside Aroumd</b> and <b>Under the river</b> - none longer than 1.4 km but steep and rough, threading between houses, walnut terraces and livestock. The big days start elsewhere: <b>Downhill Ouirgane</b> falls 909 m over 8.0 km into the Ouirgane valley, <b>Crazy Horse</b> loses 802 m over 7.3 km further west, and <b>Hwy 65</b>, <b>The String</b> and the <b>Tizi n'Oudite</b> pass traverse run north through the Asni corridor. Surfaces are dry rock, loose shale, hardpack and dust; nothing is built, bermed or bridged.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift of any kind. The normal pattern is a guided vehicle shuttle from Imlil to a pass such as Tizi Mezzik at about 2280 m, then a descent back towards the village; the guide associations in Imlil and operators out of Marrakech run these as day trips and multi-day tours. Riding the remote lines unguided means self-supported navigation in Berber-speaking mountain villages with no signage, so the shuttle-and-guide model is not just an upsell here. Prices were not verified for this map.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Remote high-mountain terrain with exposure, livestock and pedestrians. The village lines run through inhabited, cultivated land - ride them slowly and with permission, not as race runs. Carry water, spares and offline navigation.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Open from 1 April; closed from 1 December (typical riding window). Snow lingers on the high passes into spring and the valleys are very hot in midsummer; spring and autumn are best.<br/>Daily opening hours: daylight only<br/><small>Five of the drawn lines are not at Imlil itself: Downhill Ouirgane and Crazy Horse lie roughly 25 km and 45 km west, and Hwy 65, The String and Tizi n'Oudite run through the Asni corridor to the north. They are grouped here because Imlil is the base they are shuttled from. The neighbouring Oukaimeden plateau is a separate spot on this map. Nothing here is waymarked or maintained for gravity riding, and route conditions change after storms and road works.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Marrakech</td><td>Car / shuttle</td><td>~1h30</td></tr><tr><td>Asni</td><td>Car / shuttle</td><td>~30 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: all twelve trail lines were drawn in gpx.studio and supplied for this map, then simplified to ~8 m; they are not operator exports. Pin on the OpenStreetMap village node <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/87828616">87828616</a> ("Imlil"), measured at 1720 m. Shuttle and tour pattern from the <a href="https://bureaudesguidesimlil.com/mountain-biking/">Imlil guides association</a> and Marrakech-based operators. Verify prices and conditions locally.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-7.920760,31.136820,1720</coordinates></Point>
