  <Placemark>
    <name>Big Bend Ranch State Park - Sauceda (Texas) [5 USD entry]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Sauceda 1273 m - ranger station and bunkhouse in the basin between Oso Mountain and Fresno Peak, Texas</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The biggest and emptiest riding in Texas: 238 miles of multi-use trail across the Chihuahuan Desert, and the only IMBA Epic in the state. The interior basin around Sauceda sits between <b>Oso Mountain</b> (1546 m, west) and <b>Fresno Peak</b> (1544 m, east), and the routes that link them are what most riders come for - Oso Loop and Guale Mesa Road under Oso Mountain, Encino Trail and the Power Line Trail across the flats, Horsetrap Trail and Los Ojitos Road out of Sauceda itself. They are rolling desert doubletrack and singletrack rather than descents: hard-packed flow, dry creek beds, barely used 4x4 track and short technical rocky pitches, strung together over distances that make water the limiting factor. The 59-mile Fresno-Sauceda Loop chains them into the Epic; the 12-mile Encino Loop and the 9.4-mile Fresno Divide Loop are the manageable days.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No uplift, and nothing to shuttle with. Park entry 5 USD per day for 13 and over, free for children. Bunkhouse and lodge rooms at Sauceda, plus backcountry campsites; commercial outfitters in Terlingua and Lajitas rent bikes and guide trips.<br/><br/>
    <p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Remote desert. The only way into the interior is 27 miles of rough dirt road from FM 170 - high clearance strongly advised. There is no water, no shade and no phone signal on these trails, and the nearest help is the ranger station at Sauceda. Summer temperatures pass 100&deg;F by late morning and can reach 130&deg;F in the sun; ride here in the cool months. Register at Sauceda before you go out.</p>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>The park is open year round, but riding is realistically from 1 October to the end of April.<br/>
    <small>The season shown is a rider's window, not a closure: the park itself never closes, but summer heat here is a genuine hazard rather than a discomfort.</small><br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Lajitas (via FM 170 + 27 mi dirt)</td><td>Car, high clearance</td><td>~2 h</td></tr><tr><td>Presidio</td><td>Car, high clearance</td><td>~2 h</td></tr><tr><td>Alpine</td><td>Car</td><td>~3 h</td></tr></table><br/>
    <small>Pin on the Sauceda Lodge bunkhouse mapped in OpenStreetMap (<a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/564449109">way 564449109</a>), measured at 1273 m.</small><br/><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/big-bend-ranch">Texas Parks &amp; Wildlife - Big Bend Ranch State Park</a>, <a href="https://www.friendsofbigbendranch.com/resources/mountain-biking/">Friends of Big Bend Ranch - mountain biking</a> and <a href="https://www.mtbproject.com/directory/8010385/big-bend-ranch-state-park">MTB Project</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
